# Width - the stereo image, which nothing else in the rack could touch.
#
# `width` scales the side against the mid: nothing is mono, one is unchanged,
# two is twice as wide as it was recorded. It is also a diagnostic - a machine
# that has quietly gone mono is obvious the moment widening it does nothing.
#
# Two extras, both of them what mastering actually does here. `below` returns
# everything under a frequency to the centre, because width in the bass is what
# makes a mix fall apart on a big system and a phone speaker alike. `haas`
# delays one side by up to twenty milliseconds, which is width the side channel
# cannot give you: it is the ear's arrival-time cue rather than a level trick,
# and it survives a fold to mono as a comb rather than as silence.
#
# Families: width, bass, haas.

machine fx.Width

patch Init family=width

patch Narrow family=width
  # Halfway to mono. What you do to a part that is fighting the middle of the
  # mix rather than removing it.
  width          0.5

patch Mono family=width
  # The side gone entirely. Useful for checking what survives a fold, and for
  # the bass part that should never have been wide.
  width          0.0

patch Wide family=width
  # Half again as wide. About as far as this can go before the middle starts to
  # feel hollow, which is the honest limit of a mid-side trick.
  width          1.5

patch Huge family=width
  # The top of the range, with the bass pinned to the centre so the low end
  # does not come apart with the rest of it - which is what makes this usable
  # rather than merely wide.
  width          2.0
  below          140 Hz

patch "Tight Bass" family=bass
  # The width left alone and everything under two hundred hertz returned to the
  # middle. The most boring patch here and the one a mix engineer would reach
  # for first.
  width          1.0
  below          200 Hz

patch Haas family=haas
  # Twelve milliseconds on one side. The ear takes arrival time as direction,
  # so this reads as wide even though both channels carry the same thing - and
  # unlike a level trick, it leaves something behind when folded to mono.
  width          1.0
  haas           12 ms
  gain           4.4 dB

patch "Off Centre" family=haas
  # A short delay and the whole image turned, so the source sits to one side
  # and leans. For putting two takes of the same part in different places
  # without panning either of them flat.
  width          1.2
  haas           6 ms
  rotate         -0.35
  gain           3.1 dB
